# USB cameras: only their mirror may open them.
#
# A V4L2 camera streams to one reader, so the first page to open one would
# hold it against everyone else. tessaro-camera@<node>.service opens each
# real camera and republishes it as a v4l2loopback device, which any number
# of readers can open; the real node is taken away from everyone but root.
#
# A rule and not a group: 50-udev-default.rules puts every video4linux node
# in `video`, which weston is in for the GPU and for the Pi's hardware
# decoders, and a new group of our own would need an /etc/group line. The
# image has no sysusers step, and /etc/group lives on the /data overlay,
# where a device's first write shadows the image's copy forever.
#
# SUBSYSTEMS=="usb" is what limits this to USB cameras: the Pi's decoder and
# ISP nodes (/dev/video10 and up) are platform devices and keep their
# `video` group, and so do the v4l2loopback nodes the mirrors make, whose
# parent is no USB device either. It covers a UVC camera's metadata node
# as well, which nothing needs.
#
# The mode alone does not do it: systemd's 70-uaccess.rules tags every
# video4linux node `uaccess`, and 73-seat-late.rules then gives the user of
# the active seat session an ACL on it. weston.service runs in such a session
# (PAMName=login), so it could open the camera despite the 0600, and
# Chromium would list the real camera beside its virtual one and, being
# first, pick it. TAG-= takes the tag off before 73 reads it.
#
# 71-: after systemd's 50-udev-default.rules, whose GROUP= this overrides,
# 60-persistent-v4l.rules, which imports ID_V4L_CAPABILITIES from v4l_id,
# and 70-uaccess.rules; before 73-seat-late.rules.
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", OWNER="root", GROUP="root", MODE="0600", \
    TAG-="uaccess"

# The hidden mirror presence detection reads: root's alone, like the camera,
# so the page neither lists it nor takes it before tessaro-vision does.
# tessaro-camera names it `<camera> Vision` (mirrors.rs); v4l2loopback copies
# the label into the node's name before it registers it
# (`v4l2_loopback_add`), so this sees it on the add event. devtmpfs makes
# the node root 0600 before udev runs, so it is never open to weston.
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTR{name}=="*Vision", OWNER="root", GROUP="root", MODE="0600", \
    TAG-="uaccess"

# One mirror per node that captures video. v4l_id writes the capabilities as
# ":capture:" for a camera; a UVC metadata node has none of the ones it
# lists and gets ":", so it starts nothing. BindsTo= in the unit stops the
# mirror when the camera is unplugged. Not limited to ACTION=="add": a
# change event without the systemd tag would take the device unit down,
# and the mirror with it.
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ENV{ID_V4L_CAPABILITIES}==":capture:", \
    TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="tessaro-camera@%k.service"
